his possession pattern describes the present; use had for past possession. Focus on **short answer** in this question.","explanation":"Use they have in the short answer and leave out got.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"short-answer","contextKey":"possession-49","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-have-got"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"1d557c64aabf94b5aa793676d17d77e08e1d533f2e7accb7449de48fb0eee0fa","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **short answer**. Short answers keep have or has but leave out got. This possession pattern describes the present; use had for past possession.","Use they have in the short answer and leave out got.","Choose **have**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A short answer keeps **have/has** and drops got. Got alone does not supply the required auxiliary. Use they have in the short answer and leave out got.","B":"**is** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. Use they have in the short answer and leave out got.","C":"Use they have in the short answer and leave out got.","D":"**has** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. Use they have in the short answer and leave out got."}}},{"id":"73e7efc5-195d-57f4-a0c0-76cd54bec355","topicSlug":"have-got","level":"A1","prompt":"The suitcase ____ got a broken handle. Describe present possession or circumstances.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"have"},{"id":"B","text":"is"},{"id":"C","text":"does"},{"id":"D","text":"has"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Use have got with I, you, we and they; use has got with he, she, it or a singular noun. Focus on **agreement** in this question.","explanation":"Use has got with “the suitcase”: the suitcase has got a broken handle.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"agreement","contextKey":"possession-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-have-got"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"87508a78922d2356b9e418dfe4575ad5da07f6e28478eb7ea0438136c0f5bf89","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **agreement**. Use have got with I, you, we and they; use has got with he, she, it or a singular noun.","Use has got with “the suitcase”: the suitcase has got a broken handle.","Choose **has**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**have** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. Use has got with “the suitcase”: the suitcase has got a broken handle.","B":"**is** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. Use has got with “the suitcase”: the suitcase has got a broken handle.","C":"This construction uses its existing **has** form, so **does** introduces the wrong auxiliary. Use has got with “the suitcase”: the suitcase has got a broken handle.","D":"Use has got with “the suitcase”: the suitcase has got a broken handle."}}},{"id":"91eb0377-bbb5-50a4-a2bf-96baba8591a1","topicSlug":"have-got","level":"A1","prompt":"Use have got in the negative: The suitcase ____ got a broken handle.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"is not"},{"id":"B","text":"has not"},{"id":"C","text":"have not"},{"id":"D","text":"does not"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Put not after have or has. In questions, move have or has before the subject and keep got after it. Focus on **negative** in this question.","explanation":"Put not after has; do not introduce do or does into the have got pattern.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"negative","contextKey":"possession-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-have-got"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"8063ebae64a4caa0bb5ae37b216f596241156dbec02d878c08c0b94b78b90cce","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **negative**. Put not after have or has. In questions, move have or has before the subject and keep got after it.","Put not after has; do not introduce do or does into the have got pattern.","Choose **has not**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**is** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. Put not after has; do not introduce do or does into the have got pattern.","B":"Put not after has; do not introduce do or does into the have got pattern.","C":"**have** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. Put not after has; do not introduce do or does into the have got pattern.","D":"This construction uses its existing **has** form, so **does not** introduces the wrong auxiliary. Put not after has; do not introduce do or does into the have got pattern."}}},{"id":"e8780137-c7a9-5b12-aab4-bcf3e3a8192c","topicSlug":"have-got","level":"A1","prompt":"____ the suitcase got a broken handle?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Has"},{"id":"B","text":"Have"},{"id":"C","text":"Does"},{"id":"D","text":"Is"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Put not after have or has. In questions, move have or has before the subject and keep got after it. Focus on **question** in this question.","explanation":"In this question, has comes before “the suitcase”; got stays after it.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"question","contextKey":"possession-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-have-got"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"636f54102f44e5d06a8a1ec17b8b16b42ee9651fdb9aef09c17b50ca7353ec30","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **question**. Put not after have or has. In questions, move have or has before the subject and keep got after it.","In this question, has comes before “the suitcase”; got stays after it.","Choose **Has**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"In this question, has comes before “the suitcase”; got stays after it.","B":"**have** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. In this question, has comes before “the suitcase”; got stays after it.","C":"This construction uses its existing **has** form, so **Does** introduces the wrong auxiliary. In this question, has comes before “the suitcase”; got stays after it.","D":"**is** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. In this question, has comes before “the suitcase”; got stays after it."}}},{"id":"6775003e-cd09-591e-ad4f-6c2c2563546d","topicSlug":"have-got","level":"A1","prompt":"“Has the suitcase got a broken handle?” Answer positively using “it”: “Yes, it ____.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"have"},{"id":"B","text":"got"},{"id":"C","text":"is"},{"id":"D","text":"has"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Short answers keep have or has but leave out got. This possession pattern describes the present; use had for past possession. Focus on **short answer** in this question.","explanation":"Use it has in the short answer and leave out got.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"short-answer","contextKey":"possession-50","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-have-got"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v2","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"3a290b8dac32bc6852cad99b620b237b1c8d90b236c68aefbfc8e1866a8deef0","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **short answer**. Short answers keep have or has but leave out got. This possession pattern describes the present; use had for past possession.","Use it has in the short answer and leave out got.","Choose **has**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**have** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. Use it has in the short answer and leave out got.","B":"A short answer keeps **have/has** and drops got. Got alone does not supply the required auxiliary. Use it has in the short answer and leave out got.","C":"**is** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. Use it has in the short answer and leave out got.","D":"Use it has in the short answer and leave out got."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Have got is common, especially in spoken British English, for current possessions, relationships and characteristics. In this pattern have or has acts as the question-forming auxiliary. It is not the same as every use of ordinary have, nor does it normally describe a repeated action."],"rules":[{"title":"Have or has","explanation":"Use have got with I, you, we and they; use has got with he, she, it or a singular noun.","examples":["I have got a warm coat.","The cabin has got two bedrooms.","She **has got** three cousins."],"pattern":"I **have got** / She **has got**","paragraphs":["Have goes with I, you, we and they; has goes with he, she, it and a singular noun. The possessed object does not control the verb: She has got two keys still uses has because she is singular. Contractions such as I’ve got and she’s got are common."]},{"title":"Negative and question forms","explanation":"Put not after have or has. In questions, move have or has before the subject and keep got after it.","examples":["We have not got a spare wheel.","Has the flat got a balcony?","**Have** you **got** a spare charger?"],"pattern":"**Has** she **got** a key? / She **has not got** a key.","paragraphs":["To make a question, move have/has before the subject and retain got. To make a negative, put not after have/has. Ordinary have can instead use do-support: Does she have a key? Both systems are valid, but do not mix them into Does she have got a key?"]},{"title":"Short answers and time","explanation":"Short answers keep have or has but leave out got. This possession pattern describes the present; use had for past possession.","examples":["Yes, it has.","They had a garden before they moved.","They **had** a garden before they moved."],"pattern":"Yes, she **has**. / No, she **has not**.","paragraphs":["Short answers omit got and keep the appropriate auxiliary. This possession construction normally refers to now. For earlier possession, use had: I had a bicycle as a child. Future possession commonly uses will have."]},{"title":"Contrasts and edge cases","explanation":"Have got to can express obligation and is a separate pattern: I’ve got to leave. Ordinary have also appears in activities such as have breakfast; possession-style have got is not a universal replacement. Have you got ...? and Do you have ...? are both standard questions.","examples":["She **has got** three cousins.","**Have** you **got** a spare charger?","They **had** a garden before they moved."]}],"recap":["I **have got** / She **has got**","**Has** she **got** a key? / She **has not got** a key.","Yes, she **has**. / No, she **has not**."]},"stage":"1 · Sentence foundations","prerequisites":["there-is-there-are"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
